{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"So the dreamed jobs are: 1, 5, 6, 9, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 22. The completed (all success) but NOT dreamed jobs would be: 24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 29, 30, 32, 33, 34 (job_35 has task7 running so NOT all success).\n\nSo the dream --dry-run output `[job_24 job_25 job_26 job_27 job_28]` correctly lists the first 5 completed-not-dreamed jobs sorted ascending. This confirms the current selectPendingJobs logic works.\n\nThis means the dream scan filter test is currently PASSING (already implemented in cmd/dream.go). But after task7, this logic will move to handler.Dream + workspace scanning functions. The test is about verifying the behavior stays correct.\n\nNow, the key question: what should the test script assert for the dream boundary test?\n\nThe test method says: \"dream 只列「完成且未 dream」的 job（排除未完成/已 dream，按 job 号升序截断）\".\n\nSo the test should:\n1. Set up a fixture with:\n   - A \"all success\" job (not dreamed) → should be listed\n   - A \"not done\" job (some task pending/running) → should be excluded\n   - An \"already dreamed\" job → should be excluded\n2. Run `dream --dry-run`\n3. Verify the output lists only completed-not-dreamed jobs, sorted ascending, truncated.\n\nBut the tricky part: the repo's .rick already has MANY jobs. If I create fixtures in .rick/jobs, they'd be mixed with real jobs. Also, I need to be careful to not break the repo.\n\nThe best approach for an idempotent test is to use an ISOLATED .rick directory. But how does the CLI find the .rick dir? It uses `workspace.GetRickDir()` which is likely `<cwd>/.rick`.\n\nLet me check how workspace.GetRickDir works."}}
