_9 (sorted). With default job_num=5, all 3 listed: `[job_1 job_5 job_9]`.\n\nTo test truncation, I'd add more jobs. But let me keep it simpler: I'll test truncation by creating 6 completed-not-dreamed jobs and running `dream --dry-run` (default job_num=5), verifying only 5 listed and the highest excluded.\n\nActually, let me design to cover all aspects cleanly:\n\nFixture jobs:\n- job_1: success → include\n- job_2: success → include\n- job_3: success + dreamed → exclude\n- job_4: pending (one task \"running\") → exclude\n- job_5: success → include\n- job_6: success → include\n- job_7: success → include\n- job_8: success → include  (this makes 6 completed-not-dreamed jobs: 1,2,5,6,7,8)\n\nWith default job_num=5, expected list sorted ascending truncated to 5: [job_1 job_2 job_5 job_6 job_7]. job_8 excluded (6th). job_3 excluded (dreamed). job_4 excluded (pending).\n\nThis covers: exclude pending (job_4), exclude dreamed (job_3), sort ascending (job_1 job_2 job_5...), truncate (job_8 dropped).\n\nFor the learning \"tasks.json not found\" test, I need a job with doing/ but no tasks.json. job_9/doing/ (empty). \n\nFor ctrl \"doing directory not found\" test, I need a job that doesn't exist at all: use `ctrl --dry-run --job job_999`.\n\nFor human-loop dry-run, just run `human-loop --dry-run '测试主题'` (doesn't need fixture jobs; it uses draft dir). But it uses `workspace.GetDraftDir()` and `builder.BuildHumanLoop`. In isolated fixture, draft dir = fixture/.rick/draft (may not exist). BuildHumanLoop uses draftDir/rfcDir as placeholders, so it should work without the dirs existing (dry-run doesn't mkdir). Let me verify.\n\nActually, human-loop dry-run in the isolated fixture: `handler.HumanLoop` (after migration) does GetDraftDir → NextLoopID → mkdir dirs → BuildHumanLoop → dry-run prints. Wait, in the current cmd code, dry-run path:\n1. GetDraftDir\n2. NextLoopID(draftDir) → reads draft/loops (may not exist → returns loop_1)\n3. mkdir dirs (draftDir, rfcDir, concepts, human-learning, loops)\n4. BuildHumanLoop → print content\n\nIn isolated fixture, this would CREATE dirs in fixture/.rick/draft. That's fine (temp dir).\n\nBut wait, after migration to handler.HumanLoop, the orchestration order changes: GetDraftDir → NextLoopID → mkdir → BuildHumanLoop → env.Ensure → CallCLI. For dry-run, it should skip CallCLI and print. The test method says human-loop dry-run output contains `sense_loop`. This should hold regardless.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether to run human-loop in the isolated fixture or in the real repo. The human-loop dry-run writes to `.rick/draft` (creates dirs). If I run it in the real repo, it pollutes `.rick/draft/loops` and `.rick/draft/rfc` etc. But actually those dirs already exist in the real repo (there's `.rick/draft/loops/loop_6/` etc.). Running dry-run in real repo would call NextLoopID and mkdir, but since dry-run doesn't actually write loop files (BuildHumanLoop only renders in memory), it just creates empty dirs that already exist. Low pollution.\n\nBut to be safe and idempotent, I'll run ALL behavioral tests in the isolated fixture dir. This is cleaner.\n\nHowever, there's a concern: `env.Ensure` and `config.LoadConfig`. Let me check what human-loop dry-run does after migration. The task7 spec: \"handler.HumanLoop: GetDraftDir → NextLoopID → 建目录 → builder.BuildHumanLoop（产 method+instance）→ env.Ensure → runtime.CallCLI\". For dry-run, presumably it skips env.Ensure + CallCLI and just prints. But the current cmd code does dry-run check BEFORE config.LoadConfig. So dry-run should not need config.\n\nBut wait — after migration, will the dry-run still work without config? The current cmd code:\n```go\nif GetDryRun() {\n    _, content, err := pb.BuildHumanLoop(...)\n    fmt.Fprint(cmd.OutOrStdout(), content)\n    return nil\n}\n```\nSo dry-run doesn't load config. After migration to handler.HumanLoop(topic, opts), the handler should check opts.DryRun and print without config. This is the expected behavior. The test just checks output contains sense_loop and exit 0.\n\nLet me also verify: does `config.LoadConfig()` need a config file? The real repo has `config.example.json` but maybe not `config.json` or `~/.rick/config.json`. For dry-run tests, config isn't loaded. For the exception tests (learning/ctrl), config IS loaded (or not?). Let me check: learning job_N (non-dry-run) → collectExecutionData → but first it checks doing dir. Actually in the current code, `executeLearningWorkflow` → `collectExecutionData` which does NOT load config. It only loads config later in callAgentForAnalysis. So the \"tasks.json not found\" error happens before config is loaded. Good.\n\nFor ctrl --dry-run --job job_999: runCtrlDryRun → SaveCtrlPrompt → GenerateCtrlPromptFile → \"doing directory not found\" error before config. Good, no config needed.\n\nSo all behavioral tests can run in the isolated fixture without config. \n\nBut wait, there's a subtlety with env.Ensure after migration. The task7 spec says the orchestration includes env.Ensure. For the human-loop non-dry-run, it would call env.Ensure (which might need config/pi). But for dry-run, it shouldn't. The test only uses dry-run for human-loop, so no env.Ensure needed.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the human-loop test. The test method boundary says `human-loop --dry-run '测试主题'` output contains `sense_loop`. This is dry-run. Good.\n\nNow, one more concern: the isolated fixture approach requires the binary to resolve `.rick` from cwd. Since I run with cwd=fixture_dir, GetRickDir() = fixture_dir/.rick. \n\nBut `getRickDirName()` checks os.Args[0] binary name for `_dev` suffix. Our binary is named `rick` (not `_dev`), so `.rick`. Good. But wait, if I name the binary `/tmp/xxx/rick`, filepath.Base is \"rick\", no _dev. Good.\n\nLet me now also verify the dream --dry-run works in an isolated fixture (no loops/skills). Let me test it quickly.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_XRqpB0atgRv5wMd